# React coding style

Narrows `ts-js-node`'s coding style to React component files (`*.tsx`/`*.jsx`
only); non-component code stays with `ts-js-node`'s rule.

## Components

- Write function components; a class component only where none is possible (an error boundary).
- One component per file for anything exported and reused; a small private helper used only in its parent file can stay local.
- Name the file after the exported component (`UserCard.tsx`); `PascalCase` components, `camelCase` hooks (`useUserCard`) and functions.
- Destructure props in the signature.
- Type props with an explicit `interface`/`type`, not `React.FC`.

## Hooks and state

- Call hooks only at the top level of a component or hook; do not add an eslint-disable to route around the react-hooks rule (see `rules/security.mdc` and `react-build-fix`'s guardrails).
- Branch inside a hook's own logic (early return after the hook call), not around the hook call.
- Prefer several focused `useState` calls over one state object when fields update independently; group (or `useReducer`) only when they change together.
- Name a custom hook's return values by caller meaning (`{ data, isLoading, error }`).

## Imports and structure

- Group imports React/framework, third-party, local, as in `ts-js-node`'s coding-style rule.
- Import hooks by name (`import { useState } from "react"`); import the `React` namespace only if the project's JSX transform requires it (classic transform yes, automatic transform from React 17 no).
- Co-locate a component's styles, tests, and small subcomponents under one directory when the project already does; match the existing convention.
